  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-18",
    "title": "A Medium-Sized Solar Flare",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  What a  flare for the chromatic !  Today’s image  captures a piece of the  Sun ’s  chromosphere , the  layer of the Sun ’s atmosphere above its surface where the temperature  rises . Why would the Sun become hotter farther away from its  core ? This is potentially due to energy from the swirling  magnetic fields  extending up through the Sun’s atmosphere. Those same magnetic fields can  twist and snap , releasing light and energetic particles as a  solar flare . The  H-alpha filter  used for today’s image captures light from energized hydrogen atoms as they travel off the Sun’s surface during a flare on May 14, 2025. This flare was  classified  as an M1 based on  GOES   observations , meaning it was moderately bright in  X-rays . M-class flares can cause  radio blackouts  at Earth’s poles and minor  radiation storms  that can harm astronauts. They aren’t even  the most powerful flares ! NASA,  NOAA , and other agencies monitor the Sun to predict and identify such storms so we can protect humans on Earth and in space.   Tomorrow's picture:  a horse of a different color",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/solar_M_flare.jpg?w=3000&h=2759&fit=clip",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-18-an-m-class-solar-flare/",
    "credit": " Mario Cogo ",
    "alt": "A piece of the Sun takes up half the image. The Sun looks fuzzy and swirling with energetic hydrogen atoms. A bright and dark spot are visible on the Sun, associated with a sun spot. A wisp of hydrogen reaches off of the edge of the Sun.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-18T09:42:52.944351+00:00"
